Cited-References = {{[}Anonymous], FUTURE DIRECTIONS Important next steps to build on the findings presented here include further use of cross-technique comparisons for the exploration of smectite hydration. Repetition of techniques such as TGA-MS and 23Na NMR alongside RH-controlled XRD with additional swelling clays and alternate cation loadings would be insightful. Further probing of organic structures adsorbed to smectite clays including the introduction of unique functional groups and direct stereochemistry investigation, parallel to controlled moisture conditions would build on the knowledge presented here. Determination of the impact of carbohydrates synthesized for exact chain length at different orders of magnitude would be telling. Future directions may also include probing the potential for mineral-facilitated cleavage of carbohydrates. To understand better the conformations and interactions implicated in these exciting carbohydrate structure-dependent findings, molecular modeling of the systems would be beneficial. The study of flocculation and aggregation in suspensions of MONT loaded with different carbohydrate structures could also be an area of future study. Overall, the above-mentioned subject areas, as well as further points of consideration presented in Chapters 1-3, exist for further contribution to the organo-mineral field in years to come. ACKNOWLEDGMENTS Firstly, thank you to Dr. Ludmilla Aristilde for all the past and present kindness, guidance, support, scientific insight, and motivation you have furnished me with during my graduate tenure. I have truly grown both personally and as a researcher under your tutelage. I want to express huge gratitude to my committee members, Dr. Murray McBride and Dr. Taryn Bauerle for their insights and intellectual contributions that have shaped this work. To Dr. Randall Youngman at Corning Inc, thank you for always being willing to go above and beyond to test a new idea or to look into a new project. There always seemed to be one more sample condition to add on, and I never heard a word of exasperation! I also would not be where I am without the time and knowledge dedicated to me from Dr. Bruno Lanson, Dr. Eric Ferrage, and Dr. Laurent Charlet. I feel lucky to have learned from each of you. I am grateful to Dr. Alex Fernandez-Martinez and Valerie Magnin at the Institut des Sciences de la Terre (ISTerre) at the Universite Grenoble Alpes for, respectively, assistance with the FTIR measurements and conducting the water adsorption isotherms. Thank you to Kim Sparks of the Cornell University Stable Isotope Laboratory, Tatyana Dokuchayeva at the Cornell Nutrient Analysis Laboratory, and Andrew Masterson at the Stable Isotope Laboratory of the Department of Earth and Planetary Sciences at Northwestern University for helping me with sample measurements. This work made use of the IMSERC PCM facility at Northwestern University, which has received support from the Soft and Hybrid Nanotechnology Experimental (SHyNE) Resource (NSF ECCS-2025633), and from Northwestern University. I wish to thank both Dr. Christos Malliakas and Dr. Yuyang Wu of the IMSERC facility at Northwestern for their experimental guidance and technical expertise. I am thankful to my funding sources both at Cornell University and Northwestern University including the NSF Graduate Research and Training (IGERT) program, the National Science Foundation (Dr. Ludmilla Aristilde CAREER award, etc.), the Chateaubriand Fellowship from the Embassy of France in the United States, the Cornell Graduate School, and the Mario Einaudi Center for International Studies at Cornell University. Thank you to my lab mates, past and present. It has been more than any graduate student could ask for working alongside such brilliant and talented people. Special shout out to Rebecca Wilkes for being with me since Day 1 of the graduate program and going through even step of the PhD journey together. It does not seem real, but we made it!! Thank you to my family for steadfast support and unconditional love. Thank you to Carolyn Sutter for being the best role model any women could ask for. Thank you to Bruce Sutter for never letting my car go a winter unwaxed or unmaintained. Thank you to Marty Kelch for calling to inform me about every potential weather event that approached the Ithaca or Chicago area. Thank you to Sarah Mellor for the commiseration, laughs, and overall keeping me down to Earth. Thank you to my cat Tabby for traveling with me to several European countries for my research and extracting no retribution from my person. Lastly, thank you to Dakota Price; there is no me without you..
