Month = {JUL},
Abstract = {National surveys of municipal urban foresters provide an opportunity to
   summarize and benchmark urban forestry programs across municipalities.
   Such surveys - often termed ``urban forest censuses{''} - have been
   conducted internationally, including in Canada, the United States,
   Ireland, New Zealand, Sweden, and the United Kingdom. To encourage the
   expansion of such surveys, this Short Communication details the methods
   used in the 2025 Canadian Urban Forest Census (French: Recensement des
   forets urbaines canadiennes), which obtained a 40\% response rate among
   all Canadian urban municipalities, cities, regions, and towns. Designed
   with the support of urban forestry researchers across Canada, our
   bilingual, online survey was disseminated using a mixed-mode approach
   that included promotion on a national urban forest electronic mailing
   list and LinkedIn; distribution of the online survey via a QR code on
   mailed postcards; and distribution and redistribution of the online
   survey link through email. We found that distribution and redistribution
   through email was the most successful approach, with few responses
   initiated through postal mail invitations. A higher response rate was
   achieved when we could obtain direct contact information for urban
   foresters or directors of parks, public works, planning, or related
   departments. Our results suggest that piloting surveys with municipal
   foresters and prioritizing emailbased dissemination to identified
   contacts are key strategies for achieving high-quality, cost-effective
   response rates in urban forest management surveys.},
